🧠 First — Why do we even need any of this?

HTTP is stateless by nature. That means every request is like the server has AMNESIA 🤯 — it forgets you the SECOND the response is sent. So when you log in, then click to another page, the server would normally go "Who are you? Never seen you before in my life."

Cookies, Sessions, and JWTs all exist to solve this ONE problem: "How do I remember who you are across multiple requests?"

🍪 Cookies — The "sticky note" the browser keeps

A cookie is just a small piece of data the server tells the browser to store, and the browser automatically sends it back with EVERY future request.

Server: "Here, stick this note on yourself: userID=101"
Browser: [saves it] "Okay, I'll show this note every time I visit you again"

Think of it like a wristband you get at a club/event 🎪 — once they stamp it on you, you don't need to show your ID again, you just flash the wristband.

Key point: Cookies are just a STORAGE MECHANISM (a place to keep small data in the browser). They're not "login" by themselves — they can hold ANY data (login info, preferences, tracking IDs, cart items, etc).

🗄️ Sessions — The "server remembers everything, cookie just holds a claim ticket"

Now here's where it gets interesting. Session-based auth works like this:

Step 1: You log in ✅
Step 2: Server creates a "session" — a little file/record in ITS OWN memory/database that says:

Session ID: abc123  →  { userID: 101, name: "Raj", loggedIn: true }

Step 3: Server sends back JUST the session ID (abc123) as a cookie to your browser.
Step 4: Browser stores that cookie, and sends abc123 back with every request.
Step 5: Server looks up abc123 in ITS database each time → finds your full info → knows it's you! 🎯

🎬 Real-life analogy: Coat Check at a Fancy Restaurant 🧥

You hand your coat to the coat-check guy → he gives you a small numbered TICKET (like #47) → HE keeps your actual coat in the back room.

The ticket (#47) = the Session ID (cookie) — small, meaningless by itself
Your actual coat + details = stored SAFELY on the server's side, not with you

Every time you want your coat back, you just show ticket #47, and the guy looks it up. The server does all the remembering — you just carry the ticket.

🎫 JWT (JSON Web Token) — The "self-contained ID card"

JWT flips the whole approach. Instead of the server storing anything, the token ITSELF contains all your info, digitally signed so it can't be faked.

Step 1: You log in ✅
Step 2: Server creates a JWT — literally encodes your info INSIDE the token:

JWT: eyJhbGc...{userID: 101, name: "Raj", role: "user"}...signature

Step 3: Server sends this JWT to your browser (stored in localStorage or cookie).
Step 4: Browser sends this JWT back with every request (usually in a header: Authorization: Bearer <JWT>).
Step 5: Server just VERIFIES the signature (doesn't need to look anything up in a database!) → trusts what's written inside → done! ✅

🎬 Real-life analogy: A Government-issued ID Card 🪪

Your Aadhaar/ID card has your name, DOB, photo — ALL printed directly ON the card itself. When you show it, the checker doesn't need to call up some government database to confirm who you are — the card is self-verifying (it has an official seal/hologram that proves it's not fake).

That's JWT — no server-side lookup needed, everything's baked INTO the token itself, and the signature proves it wasn't tampered with.